```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) platform supporting multiple users, role hierarchy, role delegation and revocation, concurrent request processing, and enforcement of security clearance levels (High, Medium, Low) for users and objects. Administrators (a subset of High-clearance users) oversee system operations. The system enforces authorization correctness, consistency, liveness, and safety, particularly under concurrency. Users interact by submitting requests affecting objects, with request processing subject to clearance and object classification constraints. The Emergency Module is a prospective component that introduces a special Emergency mode with altered operational policies, activated and deactivated under specific conditions and with unique behaviors and privileges during its active period. Delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode are revoked upon Emergency mode exit; delegated roles assigned before Emergency mode are not affected. Only one session may be in Emergency mode at any time. Emergency mode may persist across multiple time steps but must always terminate in Normal mode.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- The system may only be in one operational mode (Normal or Emergency) at any time. Only one session may be in Emergency mode at a time.
- Emergency mode activation requires exactly two distinct administrators to jointly trigger it.
- Emergency mode duration is limited and finite; the system must eventually return to Normal mode. Emergency mode may persist across multiple time steps but cannot be permanent.
- Request Processing:
    - In Normal mode:
        - Requests must be processed strictly in FIFO (first-in, first-out) order.
        - The processor of each request must always have strictly higher clearance than both the request's classification and the submitter's clearance (High > Medium > Low).
        - The processor may never process their own requests.
        - The identity of the request processor must be recorded for every processed request.
        - The system must ensure all requests are eventually processed (liveness).
        - The submitter of a request must have clearance at least as high as the request's classification.
    - In Emergency mode:
        - All pending requests with the highest classification level among pending requests must be processed simultaneously (in a single transition), in the priority order High > Medium > Low. Once all requests at that level are processed, the next highest level is processed in the same way, and so on, until all requests are processed.
        - During Emergency mode, the processing order is determined strictly by classification: all higher-classification requests (High > Medium > Low) must be processed concurrently before any lower-classification requests, regardless of the time of request submission, with no exceptions. FIFO order is fully overridden by classification priority during Emergency mode.
        - In Emergency mode, the system must allow all higher-classification requests to be processed strictly before any lower-classification requests, and this must be verifiable by a positive scenario.
        - FIFO order for request processing may be bypassed for high-classification requests, as described above.
        - The processor of a request must always have strictly higher clearance than both the request's classification and the submitter's clearance (High > Medium > Low). The processor may never process their own requests.
        - The identity of the request processor must be recorded for every processed request.
        - The system must ensure all requests are eventually processed (liveness).
        - The submitter of a request must have clearance at least as high as the request's classification.
- Role Assignment and Delegation:
    - Direct assignment of mutually exclusive roles to a user is prohibited at all times, in both Normal and Emergency modes.
    - Users may hold mutually exclusive roles only via delegation during Emergency mode, and such delegations must be revoked immediately after Emergency mode ends (at the first Normal state).
    - Delegated roles may be created, maintained, and revoked during Emergency mode. Delegated roles are persistent across states until their expiry or revocation. Delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode must expire (be revoked) immediately upon Emergency mode exit; delegated roles assigned before Emergency mode are not affected and retain their original expiry/revocation rules.
    - Only delegation of roles is allowed; direct assignment remains unchanged throughout all system modes.
    - A user cannot delegate a role to themselves.
    - A user can only delegate a role to another user if the delegator has clearance equal to or higher than the delegatee.
- A user cannot approve or process their own requests.
- Administrators hold additional emergency privileges during Emergency mode.
- Upon deactivation of Emergency mode:
    - Emergency privileges for administrators must be revoked.
    - All delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately upon Emergency exit. Delegated roles assigned before Emergency mode are not affected and retain their original expiry/revocation rules.
    - Each administrator must update their credentials exactly once, enforced by the system, immediately after every Emergency period (i.e., in the first Normal state after Emergency ends). Credential update is not required in Normal mode.
- Existing system liveness, safety, clearance, and delegation constraints remain unless explicitly superseded by Emergency mode requirements.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Activation
Exactly two administrators are required to jointly trigger the Emergency mode while the system is in Normal mode. Only one Emergency mode session may be active at any time.

R2: Emergency Duration and Deactivation
Emergency mode is active only for a limited, fixed period; after this period, the system must return to Normal mode. Emergency mode may persist across multiple time steps but must always terminate in Normal mode.

R3: Request Processing and Role Handling
R3.1: In Normal mode, requests must be processed strictly in FIFO order, with the identity of the processor recorded for each request. The processor must always have strictly higher clearance than both the request's classification and the submitter's clearance (High > Medium > Low), and must never process their own requests. The submitter must have clearance at least as high as the request's classification. The system must ensure all requests are eventually processed.
R3.2: In Emergency mode, all pending requests of the highest classification (High > Medium > Low) must be processed concurrently before any lower-classification requests, regardless of arrival order. The processing order during Emergency mode is strictly by classification priority: all higher-classification requests must always be processed before any lower-classification requests, with no exceptions and regardless of the time of request submission. Once all requests at that level are processed, the next highest level is processed in the same way, until all requests are processed. FIFO order is fully overridden by classification priority during Emergency mode. In Emergency mode, the system must allow all higher-classification requests to be processed strictly before any lower-classification requests, and this must be verifiable by a positive scenario. The processor of each request must always have strictly higher clearance than both the request's classification and the submitter's clearance (High > Medium > Low), must never process their own requests, and the identity of the processor must be recorded for every processed request. The submitter must have clearance at least as high as the request's classification. The system must ensure all requests are eventually processed.
R3.3: Direct assignment of mutually exclusive roles to a user is prohibited at all times. During Emergency mode, users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles only via delegation, and such delegations must be revoked immediately after Emergency mode ends (at the first Normal state). Delegated roles may be created, maintained, or revoked during Emergency mode, are persistent across states until expiry or revocation, and must expire at Emergency mode exit. Delegation is only allowed for roles, and the delegator must have clearance equal to or higher than the delegatee. A user cannot delegate a role to themselves.

R4: Emergency Privileges and Deactivation Effects
R4.1: During Emergency mode, administrators temporarily gain emergency-specific privileges.
R4.2: Upon exiting Emergency mode, all emergency privileges granted to administrators must be revoked.
R4.3: All roles delegated during the emergency period must be revoked immediately at the first Normal state after Emergency exit. Roles delegated before Emergency mode are not revoked and retain their original expiry and revocation rules.
R4.4: Each administrator must update credentials exactly once, enforced by the system, immediately after every Emergency period (i.e., in the first Normal state after Emergency ends). Credential update is not required in Normal mode.
